by javiereguiluz at 2025-03-27T08:15:20Z

About fully-stylize the components, I'd provide both individual and a combined CSS file with lots of CSS properties7variables for easy customization.

This is how it would work:

* Install the toolkit: `composer require symfony/ux-toolkit-bundle`
* Use some of the provided widgets in your templates: `<twig:ux:Button primary block>Save changes</twig:ux:Button>` (or `... type="primary" block="{{ yes }}"`)
* Add the needed styles:
  ```twig
    {# BEFORE #}
    {% block importmap %}
        {{ importmap(['app']) }}
    {% endblock %}

    {# AFTER #}
    {% block importmap %}
        {{ importmap(['app', 'ux-button']) }}
    {% endblock %}
  ```

That's it.

If you build the entire UI with the UX Toolkit widgets, adding each individual CSS style is very boring. So, we provide a CSS file with ALL the styles. You only have to do this once in some template:

  ```twig
    {% block importmap %}
        {{ importmap(['app', 'ux-toolkit']) }}
    {% endblock %}
  ```

-----

Regarding how do we let folks change the DOM / HTML structure of the widgets. We don't. This is a design system toolkit. You have a few widgets and each of them provides a bunch of configurable options and also some CSS styles with a ton of configurable properties.

If you want to change a lot of things in the HTML structure of the UX toolkit ... you are NOT using the UX Toolkit; you are using your own thing. We should not care about that use case.

